                <pb facs="0561" n="437" xml:id="pg437"/>

                <head>No. 263.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morton to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-11-23"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 23,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Have seen Mr. Gambetta and urged the abrogation of the pork decree. He says there
                    exists serious public apprehension of danger from consumption American pork,
                    which he personally does not share, but finds necessity of some reason for
                    abrogation of decree which would satisfy public, and wished to know if our
                    government could not inaugurate public inspection of <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >meats</hi> for export; this would greatly facilitate satisfactory
                    settlement. He understood other governments had made similar suggestion. He
                    expressed himself as convinced of exaggerated character of supposed danger and
                    as personally favorable to friendly action, but also as obliged to confront
                    prejudice which could not be overlooked.</p>
                <closer><signed>MORTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
